## Parser

ArduForge parses Arduino C++ with tree-sitter, and ships two WebAssembly
binaries built from these packages:

  web-tree-sitter    MIT    the tree-sitter runtime
  tree-sitter-cpp    MIT    the C++ grammar

Both are loaded as wasm at runtime. The native bindings in those packages are
not used.


## Bundled JavaScript dependencies

The browser bundle includes the project's npm dependencies. All are permissive
(predominantly MIT, with ISC, Apache-2.0 and BSD) with one exception worth
naming:

  elkjs    EPL-2.0    graph layout, used to arrange imported sketches

The Eclipse Public License 2.0 is a file-level copyleft. Depending on elkjs
unmodified imposes no obligation on ArduForge's own license. If you distribute
a prebuilt bundle rather than source, EPL-2.0 requires that the corresponding
source for elkjs remain available; it is published at
https://github.com/kieler/elkjs under that licence.


## Arduino example sketches in the import corpus

corpus/tierB/ contains sketches used to test the sketch importer against real
Arduino code rather than only against code this project generated.

Sketches sourced from the Arduino IDE's bundled examples come from
https://github.com/arduino/arduino-examples, which is released under CC0-1.0, a
public domain dedication. Most also carry an inline "This example code is in
the public domain" line of their own.

  AnalogInOutSerial, AnalogReadSerial, BareMinimum, Blink, BlinkWithoutDelay,
  Button, Calibration, Debounce, DigitalReadSerial, Fade, RowColumnScanning,
  Smoothing, StateChangeDetection, toneMelody

Sweep comes from the Arduino Servo library's examples and carries its own
public domain declaration:

  Sweep    by BARRAGAN, modified 2013 by Scott Fitzgerald
           "This example code is in the public domain."

ShiftOut is not a copy of anything. Arduino IDE 2.x no longer bundles that
example, so it was written for this project and lives as source in
scripts/build-corpus.ts.


## Arduino libraries required by component nodes

Some nodes generate code that depends on an Arduino library. ArduForge does not
bundle, vendor, or redistribute any of them. When a graph needs one,
`arduino-cli` fetches it from the Arduino Library Manager onto the user's own
machine, and it is compiled into the user's sketch. Each is governed by its own
licence, which is not affected by, and does not affect, ArduForge's.

  Adafruit NeoPixel
  DHT sensor library
  IRremote
  LiquidCrystal I2C
  RTClib
  SD
  Servo                (LGPL-2.1, per its upstream LICENSE.txt)
  SoftwareSerial
  Stepper

StandardFirmata is handled the same way. The "Upload StandardFirmata" action
compiles it from the Firmata library already installed on the user's machine
rather than shipping a prebuilt binary, so no Firmata code is redistributed
here either.


## Toolchain

ArduForge invokes `arduino-cli` as an external program. It is neither bundled
nor redistributed, and remains under its own licence.
